  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Lengthy run of dry and mild weather continues \ninto early November before coming to an end by late next week \n\nAnother very pleasant and comfortable weekend to close out \nOctober (Halloween) and start November (ending daylight saving \ntime). \n\nMajor message is that the high pressure overhead will not yield\nto anything for several more days. It deflects one system to our\nnorth early in the week before somethings stronger can gather in\nthe MS Valley and slide slowly this way. \n\nMinor messages include:\n\n Morning fog and lower clouds should burn away after a couple \nof hours today with high clouds for the rest of the day. The \ntemps will be down significantly (down 10-12F) from Fri in the \nSE, but the NW will benefit from warm advection and add 3-7F. \n\n Dewpoints should get into the upper 20s to lower 30s today and\ndrop RH into the 30s almost everywhere. There is a small \nconcern for a dewpoint bomb occurring today with very dry air \nlurking just 2-4kft up. Drastically lower dewpoints would be \nmore- likely over the central mountains and Allegheny Plateau \nthan elsewhere today. However, thankfully, the wind speed is \nnowhere near a level where we would need to consider a Red Flag \nWarning for fire weather concerns. The wind yesterday caused \nmore of a concern. With many days of dry weather (lack of \nmoisture and no precip) in store, the fire weather threat will \ncontinue to be at the top of the list of weather concerns to \nevaluate. \n\n Temps will be several degrees above normal during the daytimes\nwith maxes 10-15F above normal for days 3-7 or longer. Morning\nlows will rise more-gradually, but still reach 15-20F above\nnormal for Thurs-Fri. We could set a record maxi-min or two.",
